Why "or later"
--------------

Upstream's repository carries the GPL-2.0 text and does not say which versions
of the licence apply. Section 9 of GPL-2.0 lets a program that specifies no
version number be used under any version the Free Software Foundation has
published. Upstream's code is therefore offered here under GPL-2.0-or-later,
and so are the fork's changes and this repository's own work.

Keeping "or later" leaves a route to GPLv3 open. That matters because OpenCV is
licensed under Apache-2.0, which the FSF considers compatible with GPLv3 but
not with GPLv2.


Modified files
--------------

GPL-2.0 section 2(a) asks that modified files carry prominent notices saying
that they were changed, and when. The headers of opencv.py, pagetree.py and
.gitignore say that they derive from upstream and since when LowGuiGui has
changed them. The git history records each change and its date, and
docs/provenance/ maps the commits made before this repository existed back to
the archive.


About the AI-written code
-------------------------

The fork's changes and this repository's code were written by an AI coding
assistant (Anthropic's Claude Code) at the owner's direction, and were not
reviewed line by line by a human. Whether AI-generated output is protected by
copyright is unsettled; to the extent it is, the notices above apply. The
README says more, including what has been verified and how.
